---
/**
 * The concept index, which is really a question index.
 *
 * The 28 concept records hold 147 questions on which the Ayurvedic sources genuinely disagree, with
 * 461 attributed positions between them, and 384 statements of what is not established. That is
 * the citable asset here, not the 28 essays, and this page is built to put it in front of a reader
 * and a crawler in one place.
 *
 * Why that ordering rather than a tidy list of concepts: the site's own measurement says a
 * long-form guide earns nothing, while a precisely named thing with structured fact earns
 * citations. A sourced disagreement is structured fact. "Is agni identical with pitta, or distinct
 * from it?" is a real query with a real answer, and the answer is that the texts disagree.
 */
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import concepts from '../../data/concepts.json';
import { abs, rel, datasetIdentity } from '../../lib/site';

const data = concepts as any;
const records = data.records as any[];
const domains = data.domains as { id: string; label: string }[];
const sum = data.summary;

const title = 'Where Ayurvedic theory disagrees with itself';
const description = `${sum.contestedQuestions} questions on which the classical Ayurvedic sources `
  + `genuinely disagree, with ${sum.attributedPositions} positions given as their sources state them, `
  + `across ${sum.records} concepts. Plus ${sum.notKnownStatements} statements of what is not known.`;
const canonical = '/concept/';

const n = (x: number) => x.toLocaleString('en-GB');

/** Every contested question, with the concept it belongs to. */
const questions = records.flatMap((r) => (r.contested ?? []).map((c: any) => ({
  question: c.question,
  positions: c.positions ?? [],
  slug: r.slug,
  concept: r.title,
})));

const byDomain = domains.map((d) => ({
  ...d,
  items: records.filter((r) => r.domainGroup === d.id).sort((a, b) => a.title.localeCompare(b.title)),
}));
const unplaced = records.filter((r) => !r.domainGroup);

const jsonld: any[] = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'DefinedTermSet',
    '@id': `${abs(canonical)}#set`,
    name: 'Ayurvedic concepts',
    description,
    url: abs(canonical),
    license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
    creator: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#organization` },
    publisher: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#organization` },
    hasDefinedTerm: records.map((r) => ({
      '@type': 'DefinedTerm',
      '@id': `${abs(`/concept/${r.slug}/`)}#concept`,
      name: r.title,
      url: abs(`/concept/${r.slug}/`),
      inDefinedTermSet: { '@id': `${abs(canonical)}#set` },
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Dataset',
    ...datasetIdentity(),
    '@id': `${abs(canonical)}#dataset`,
    name: data.name,
    description: data.description,
    url: abs(canonical),
    license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    creator: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#organization` },
    publisher: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#organization` },
    variableMeasured: ['concept', 'contested question', 'attributed position', 'what is not known', 'classical citation', 'research assessment'],
    distribution: [
      { '@type': 'DataDownload', name: 'JSON', encodingFormat: 'application/json', contentUrl: abs('/concepts.json') },
      { '@type': 'DataDownload', name: 'CSV', encodingFormat: 'text/csv', contentUrl: abs('/concepts.csv') },
    ],
  },
  /**
   * The contested questions as an ItemList, NOT as an FAQPage, and the distinction matters.
   *
   * FAQPage requires the answer to be visible on the page carrying the markup. This index shows
   * the questions and sends the reader to the concept that holds the answer; the positions
   * themselves are on those pages, where each per-concept page emits its own FAQPage over its own
   * visible content. Marking this page up as an FAQPage would have asserted 147 answers it does
   * not display, and would have duplicated every one of them against the page that does.
   *
   * So this is what the page actually is: an ordered index of questions, each pointing at the
   * page that answers it. The `item` is a Question with no acceptedAnswer, because the answer is
   * at the url, not here.
   */
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'ItemList',
    '@id': `${abs(canonical)}#questions`,
    name: 'Contested questions in Ayurvedic theory',
    description: `${sum.contestedQuestions} questions on which the classical Ayurvedic sources `
      + 'disagree. Each is answered on the concept page it belongs to, by setting out the competing '
      + 'positions as their sources state them rather than by picking one.',
    numberOfItems: questions.length,
    itemListOrder: 'https://schema.org/ItemListUnordered',
    itemListElement: questions.map((q, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      url: abs(`/concept/${q.slug}/`),
      item: {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: q.question,
        answerCount: q.positions.length,
        url: abs(`/concept/${q.slug}/`),
        isPartOf: { '@id': `${abs(`/concept/${q.slug}/`)}#concept` },
      },
    })),
  },
];
---
<Page title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} updated={data.updatedAt}
  trail={[{ label: 'Concepts' }]} jsonld={jsonld}>
  <p class="answer">
    Ayurvedic theory is usually written about as though it were settled. It is not. These
    {n(sum.records)} concept records hold <strong>{n(sum.contestedQuestions)} questions on which the
    sources genuinely disagree</strong>, with {n(sum.attributedPositions)} positions given as their
    sources state them, and <strong>{n(sum.notKnownStatements)} statements of what is not
    established</strong>. Where Charaka and Sushruta differ, this says so and says which is which.
  </p>

  <p class="lede">
    No question below is answered with a pick. The answer to a contested question is the
    disagreement, attributed. Where a concept has no established physical referent, the record says
    that in those words rather than implying one.
  </p>

  <table class="facts">
    <caption>What is here</caption>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Concepts</th><td>{n(sum.records)}{sum.recordsWithheld > 0 && <>, with {sum.recordsWithheld} withheld as not yet verified</>}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Contested questions</th><td>{n(sum.contestedQuestions)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Attributed positions</th><td>{n(sum.attributedPositions)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Stated as not known</th><td>{n(sum.notKnownStatements)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Classical citations</th><td>{n(sum.classicalCitations)}, {n(sum.quotesPublished)} quoting the text</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Research papers assessed</th><td>{n(sum.researchItems)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Component terms</th><td>{n(sum.components)}, {n(sum.componentsLinkedToLexicon)} cross-linked to the <a href={rel('/lexicon/')}>terminology lexicon</a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Every contested question</h2>
  <p>
    Each links to the concept that holds it, where the competing positions are set out with their
    sources.
  </p>
  {byDomain.filter((d) => d.items.length).map((d) => (
    <>
      <h3>{d.label}</h3>
      <ul class="qlist">
        {d.items.flatMap((r) => (r.contested ?? []).map((c: any) => (
          <li>
            <a href={rel(`/concept/${r.slug}/`)}>{c.question}</a>
            <span class="in"> · {r.title}, {c.positions.length} positions</span>
          </li>
        )))}
      </ul>
    </>
  ))}

  <h2>The concepts themselves</h2>
  <p>
    Each page states how much weight the concept bears before it explains the concept, because that
    is the thing a reader most needs and least often gets.
  </p>
  {byDomain.filter((d) => d.items.length).map((d) => (
    <>
      <h3>{d.label} <span class="count">{d.items.length}</span></h3>
      <ul class="grid">
        {d.items.map((r) => (
          <li class="card">
            <a href={rel(`/concept/${r.slug}/`)}>{r.title}</a>
            <p>
              <strong>{(r.contested ?? []).length}</strong> contested,
              <strong> {(r.notKnown ?? []).length}</strong> stated unknown,
              <strong> {(r.modern ?? []).length}</strong> papers assessed.
            </p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </>
  ))}
  {unplaced.length > 0 && (
    <>
      <h3>Not yet placed in a subject area <span class="count">{unplaced.length}</span></h3>
      <p class="qlist">
        {unplaced.map((r, i) => (<>{i > 0 && ' · '}<a href={rel(`/concept/${r.slug}/`)}>{r.title}</a></>))}
      </p>
    </>
  )}

  <h2>What is withheld, and why</h2>
  <p>
    A concept publishes here only when every quoted classical citation in it has been read against
    its source and carries a verdict, and no refutation against it is outstanding.
    {sum.recordsWithheld > 0 && <> {sum.recordsWithheld} records are held back on that rule: three because a repair came back needing a human judgement, and one because part of its citation set has not been read yet.</>}
    {' '}A quotation is withheld if it cannot be cleared for publication or if nobody has checked
    it, and the citation still publishes its address and our account of it, because an address a
    reader can follow to a library is most of the value.
  </p>

  <h2>Download</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={rel('/concepts.json')}>concepts.json</a> and
    <a href={rel('/concepts.csv')}>concepts.csv</a>, CC BY 4.0. The JSON keeps the record
    structure. The CSV is one row per attributed position, {n(sum.attributedPositions)} of them,
    because the position is the atom: group the rows by question to get the per-question view back.
  </p>

  <p class="meta">
    Exported {data.updatedAt} from the concept corpus, so this cannot drift from it.
    Machine-readable twin of every concept page: append <code>.md</code> to its URL.
  </p>
</Page>

<style>
  .qlist { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 1.4rem; padding: 0; }
  .qlist li { margin-bottom: .5rem; font-size: .96rem; }
  .qlist .in { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: .86em; }
  .count { color: var(--ink-soft); font-weight: 400; font-size: .8em; }
</style>
